    "created_at": "2026-06-25T09:01:08Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-06-25T14:24:45Z",
    "body": "I'd like to discuss three options at WHATNOT:\n\n- **Option 1:** Revealing the byte size of a cross-origin valid media resource is fine. We already reveal duration, and something having the same duration and a different byte size will be very rare. \n- **Option 2:** We hide the range header value from developers, but we use it when we go to the network/http cache. This is what I've drafted in https://github.com/whatwg/fetch/pull/1937. Risks: The developer doesn't know a request is a range request, and may act incorrectly based on the false information. It becomes a tricksy part of the platform.\n- **Option 3:** As option 2, but instead of hiding the range header from the developer, we lie about the value (we just return `0-`). The developer therefore knows it's a range request. However, the incorrect value may still result in them acting incorrectly based on the false info. It's still a tricksy part of the platform.\n\n```html\n<video src=\"//other-origin/whatever.mp4\"></video>\n```\n\nIn the service worker:\n\n```js\nonfetch = (event) => {\n  console.log(event.request.headers.get('range'));\n\n  event.respondWith(fetch(event.request));\n};\n```\n\nThe results of the log assuming the range `0-500` is requested:\n\n- **Option 1:** `\"0-500\"`\n- **Option 2:** `null`\n- **Option 3:** `\"0-\"`\n\nIn all three cases a request is made with the range `0-500`.",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
