      "site_admin": false
    },
    "created_at": "2026-06-25T16:42:51Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-06-26T09:27:26Z",
    "body": "The conclusion in WHATNOT was to go with [none of the options](https://github.com/whatwg/fetch/issues/1936#issuecomment-4797544826), and instead, if the request is from media, and no-cors, it skips the service worker.\n\nThis means developers must use the `crossorigin` attribute on media elements to make them CORS requests, even if they're same origin.\n\nI'm ok with this, given the security issues, and how hacky the other options were.\n\nFirefox has, for some time now, bypassed the Service Worker for cross-origin no-cors media requests, and this is just an expansion of that.\n\nI'm curious what Chrome folks think about this.\n\nEdit: actually there's one part of this that wasn't clear to me from the meeting.\n\n- **Option 1:** All no-cors requests from media skip the service worker. This means we can remove the `range` handling from the fetch spec. Media elements must use `crossorigin` in order to go var the service worker, even if they're same-origin.\n- **Option 2:** As option 1, but allow no-cors same-origin media requests to go via the service worker. We'd need to retain the `range` handling in the fetch spec as the request can be redirected, which preserves the range header.\n",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
      "url": "https://api.github.com/repos/whatwg/fetch/issues/comments/4802023793/reactions",
